  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B001",
        "laureates": [
          "Antoine Georges",
          "Dieter Vollhardt",
          "Gabriel Kotliar"
        ],
        "citation": "For the development of dynamical mean-field theory for strongly correlated electrons."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B007",
    "B004"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Dynamical mean-field theory turns a difficult many-body problem into a clear, rigorous framework for understanding strongly correlated electrons and the Mott transition. Its sustained use across decades of theory and experiment shows that it is both foundational and mature. Georges, Vollhardt, and Kotliar are credible central architects of the method and its influential applications.",
    "case_against": "The main reservation is attribution: the framework builds on earlier ideas and a wider research community, and a three-person award risks obscuring those contributions. The committee should establish the distinct foundational roles of the proposed laureates and keep the citation focused on the method they developed.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "He sees the method as central to modern condensed-matter physics and mature, while noting that its development involved a substantial community and that credit among originators and later developers is difficult.",
        "response": "I agree that this history requires careful scrutiny. That difficulty does not erase the case for recognition if we can distinguish the central architects' contributions to the framework from its later development. The concise citation should recognize the method rather than imply sole authorship of the field."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "olsson-eva",
        "point": "She argues that ultracold atoms in optical lattices made controlled many-body systems accessible, while noting that the prize boundary spans theoretical foundations and experimental milestones and that Cirac also contributed substantially.",
        "response": "This is a strong case, particularly for the experimental access it created. I would keep it as an alternative because its broad scope and attribution across theory and several experimental milestones are less sharply bounded than the mature theoretical framework in B001."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The committee needs to establish how the original formulation of dynamical mean-field theory should be distinguished from earlier ideas and subsequent development when assigning credit."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "None apparent from the supplied profile."
  }
}
